<Base
  title="About — Stevie Thomas"
  description="Hitting coach at Driveline Baseball, working on swing mechanics, motor learning, and public baseball data."
>
  <section style="padding: clamp(2.5rem, 7vw, 4.5rem) 0 0;">
    <p class="eyebrow reveal" style="--i:0">About</p>
    <h1 class="reveal" style="--i:1">Stevie Thomas</h1>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I'm a hitting coach at Driveline Baseball in Kent, Washington. I work with a group of
        hitters day to day — assessments, programming, cage work, and the long unglamorous middle
        where a change actually sticks.
      </p>

      <p>
        The part I keep coming back to is the gap between what a hitter feels and what actually
        happened. A swing can feel identical twice and produce a barrel and a whiff. Bat tracking,
        force plates, and markerless motion capture have made that gap measurable in a way it
        wasn't ten years ago, and most of my outside-the-cage time goes into using that well —
        which usually means figuring out which numbers are load-bearing and which ones are just
        along for the ride.
      </p>

      <p>
        I build most of my own tools. Nothing here is a product; it's a coach with a Python
        install and a stubborn question.
      </p>

      <h3>The shape of the work</h3>
      <p>
        Roughly three things, and they feed each other.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Coaching.</strong> The actual job. Hitters, assessments, programs, meetings, and
          the film that goes with them. Everything else on this site exists because of a question
          that came up here first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measurement.</strong> Working out what the capture systems can and cannot tell
          you. A surprising amount of this is plumbing — formats, sampling rates, conventions — and
          a surprising amount of the value is in catching the places where a number does not mean
          what it appears to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Practice design.</strong> Turning any of it into something a hitter does on a
          Tuesday. This is the hardest of the three and the one with the least literature behind it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>What I'll talk about all day</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Bat path, attack angle, and why "swing up" is an incomplete instruction</li>
        <li>Variability in the swing as a skill rather than a defect</li>
        <li>Constraint-led drill design and what actually transfers to a game</li>
        <li>The run-value math that quietly decides how a lineup should be built</li>
        <li>Why the interesting result is usually the one that failed to replicate</li>
        <li>Whether a cue is doing work or just sounding like it is</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Opinions I hold with some confidence</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          In-session improvement is nearly worthless as evidence that a session worked. The signal
          shows up next week, when nobody is measuring.
        </li>
        <li>
          A mean swing is a swing nobody took. Most of what a hitter needs to hear is in the spread,
          not the average.
        </li>
        <li>
          Between-hitter correlations are not coaching instructions, and the slide from one to the
          other is the most common error in this field.
        </li>
        <li>
          Most models that fit do not reproduce, and almost nobody checks. Rerunning is free.
        </li>
        <li>
          If a validation step has never returned a negative, it is decoration.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Outside the cage</h3>
      <p>
        Motor learning and the ecological side of skill acquisition, which is most of what I read.
        Hardware projects that let me measure something I could previously only guess at. Video
        tooling, mostly so film can make an argument instead of a vibe. And the general problem of
        explaining a quantitative result to someone who has to act on it in four seconds while
        slightly out of breath.
      </p>

      <h3>The fine print</h3>
      <p>
        Everything on this site is either built on public data or described at the level of method.
        Athlete data, client work, and anything internal to Driveline stays off it. If a project
        page reads a little abstract, that's why — and where a project is built on work data, you
        will get the method and the conclusion without the numbers underneath.
      </p>
      <p>
        Opinions here are mine and not my employer's.
      </p>

      <h3>Get in touch</h3>
      <p>
        Easiest is <a href="https://x.com/stevie_t12">@stevie_t12</a> on X. I'm happy to talk shop
        with coaches and players, and I'd rather be told I'm wrong than agreed with politely.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
